messages:
sam_sales: Heads-up: Aurora Health POC kickoff tomorrow. Requirements from them: sustained 200 QPS, hourly 3-minute bursts at 900 QPS, multi-AZ, strict KMS + audit logs. They asked for a short runbook + cost comparison hosted vs dedicated. Can we pull together before the customer sync at 10am PT?

lee_se: On it. What model and expected tokens per call? That'll change GPU footprint and cost.

sam_sales: They said 'latency-first' so prefer a quantized 7b-ish model. Avg prompt 60 tokens, response ~40 tokens. Mostly many short chat gets.

ravi_perf: Routing plan + load phases I suggest: 1) Verify baseline - 5m at 5 QPS 2) Smoke - 2m at 50 QPS 3) Ramp - linear to 200 QPS over 5m, sustain 15m 4) Burst cycle - 3x (3m at 900 QPS with 10m cool-down) 5) AZ failover - simulate connection loss to one AZ 6) Post-test collection - logs, p50/p95/p99, CPU/GPU metrics, contention.

ravi_perf: Notes: short responses -> leverage batching but keep batch sizes small to avoid head-of-line blocking. Enable KV prefix cache for chat threads where possible.

emily_net: For multi-AZ: recommend deploying at least 2 pinned replicas per AZ or enable cross-AZ routing with sticky sessions. Pinned replicas reduce cross-AZ tail spikes. We can do either but must document config and costs.

harsha_security: Security checklist: VPC peering + route tables, customer-managed KMS, TLS mutual auth for ingestion, syslog export to their SIEM, SOC2 + pen test summary. KMS + VPC peering are mandatory. Audit logs must be available via S3 export within 24h.

sam_sales: Good. Include those in the runbook. Also client asked: p99 latency target p99 < 220ms during bursts. Is that realistic?

ravi_perf: p99<220ms at 900 QPS burst is aggressive on shared hosted. With Dedicated pinned replicas, optimized batching, and pre-warmed workers we might meet it. Recommend canary burst to verify and fallback policy (route to smaller model or reduce max_tokens).

emily_net: Quick k6 example to simulate ramp+burst (adjust env vars):

```js
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 50 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '15m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '3m', target: 900 },
    { duration: '10m', target: 0 }
  ],
  thresholds: { 'http_req_duration{type:generate}': ['p(99)<220'] }
};
export default function () {
  const body = JSON.stringify({ model: 'q7b-lat', prompt: '...60 tokens...', max_tokens: 40 });
  http.post('__API_ENDPOINT__', body, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${__TOKEN__}` } });
  sleep(0.01);
}
```

ravi_perf: Very rough cost sketch (for customer-facing quick view): assumptions: avg tokens/request = 100 (in+out). Sustained 200 QPS for 1h => 200 * 3600 * 100 = 72,000,000 tokens. Hosted placeholder rate $0.06 / 1k tokens => hosted ~= $4,320 for 1hr sustained. Dedicated estimate: reserved node w/2xA100-ish amortized ~ $520/hr (includes overhead) and gives lower per-token cost under sustained load and reliable burst headroom. These are estimates; we'll send detailed calc.

sam_sales: Good — can we turn that into a 1-slide summary for the customer with bullet cost comparisons and key risk items?

lee_se: I'll draft the one-pager. Also adding operational notes: set short connection timeouts on client side, enable client-side retries with jitter, and use exponential backoff for 429s.

harsha_security: For KMS integration timeline: once keys are provided it takes ~48-72 hours to finalize config and verification with compliance. VPC peering + firewall changes ~1 business day if they open routes quickly.

ops-bot: Infra note: capacity pool prewarm request created: pool-id cp-aurora-900, requested 2026-06-10T07:00:00Z.

sam_sales: Action items: @lee_se one-pager + runbook + k6 scripts, @ravi_perf full perf calc + exact GPU counts, @harsha_security checklist + timeline. I'll run the 10am PT sync.

ravi_perf: One more: include a fallback policy in the runbook: if p99 > 220ms for two consecutive 3-min windows during burst -> automatically route new sessions to the smaller model or enable response-compression (truncate token budget). We'll include the deploy flags.

sam_sales: Perfect. Thanks team. We'll align with Aurora at 10. :rocket:
